artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider precision studies of neutron and nuclear β decay used to test the electroweak sector of the Standard Model and to search for beyond-Standard-Model (BSM) couplings (in particular exotic scalar and tensor currents that contribute through the Fierz interference term b, as well as right-handed currents and deviations of Vud). The experimentally accessible decay systems traditionally considered are:

(i) free neutron decay;
(ii) superallowed 0+ → 0+ pure Fermi transitions between isobaric analog states;
(iii) mirror β transitions within isospin T = 1/2 doublets (mixed Fermi/Gamow–Teller);
(iv) pure Gamow–Teller transitions within T = 1 isotriplets.

The experimentally accessible observables are:
(a) integrated decay rates (ft and corrected Ft values);
(b) angular correlation coefficients (e.g. the β–ν correlation a, the β-asymmetry A, the neutrino-asymmetry B, the Fierz term b);
(c) differential β-energy spectrum shapes, in which BSM scalar/tensor couplings appear linearly via a 1/Ee Fierz-type distortion;
(d) nuclear/atomic recoil spectroscopy enabled by ion and atom traps and novel detection technologies.

Problem. Determine, among the combinations of system (i)–(iv) and observable (a)–(d), which currently provides the greatest sensitivity to the limiting unknowns of the Standard-Model description of semileptonic charged-current weak interactions and to BSM scalar, tensor, and right-handed couplings, taking into account both achievable experimental precision and the level of irreducible theoretical (e.g. nuclear-structure, radiative-correction, recoil) uncertainty in each case. Provide a comparative ranking of sensitivities and identify the dominant limiting factors for each leading combination.

